            <ArticleTitle>Meatal obstruction following canal wall down mastoidectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1450-1451</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chronic otitis media (COM) is completely treated with a single procedure in most patients but may require multiple surgeries in some cases. The main goals of open cavity mastoidectomy are to completely eradicate the disease, create a large enough meatus for examination and mastoid air cells, and provide a self-cleaning epithelized dry cavity, and achieve maximum hearing. In this report, we present a very rare case of COM who underwent revision mastoidectomy in our clinic due to meatal obstruction in the right ear, total sensorineural hearing loss, and pain and tenderness in the postauricular region.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
